cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this verifier, or cite the Signed Containment Evidence
  specification it implements, please cite it using the metadata below.
title: >-
  Signed Containment Evidence: specification and reference verifiers
abstract: >-
  This is the specification and reference implementation set for Signed
  Containment Evidence, a document format that records that a compute workload
  ran in a contained environment and was then destroyed. The evidence can be
  checked offline, by a party with no relationship to whoever produced it. An
  AuditProof carries an eight-step SHA-512 hash chain over the destruction
  sequence, together with an Ed25519 attestation over a fixed canonical view of
  the document serialised under RFC 8785.

  A verifier answers two separate questions. Integrity is whether the document
  is internally consistent and unmodified, which can be established from the
  document alone. Authenticity is whether the key that signed it belongs to the
  party named in it, which requires an external trust anchor. A document can be
  internally consistent and still be signed by a key that nobody vouches for, so
  the two results are reported separately and the exit codes distinguish them.

  The repository contains four implementations, in Rust, Go, Python and
  TypeScript, along with a shared failure taxonomy and a conformance corpus of
  100 cases in nine categories. The corpus is produced by a generator that is
  included, so its expected verdicts can be regenerated and compared rather than
  taken on trust. Corpus test suites for Rust and Go are present; runners for the
  Python and TypeScript ports are not yet included.

  Three properties hold by construction. None of the implementations has a
  network dependency, so offline verification follows from the code rather than
  from a policy. A verifier that does not implement a document's declared signing
  mode rejects the document instead of returning a partial result, which removes
  a downgrade an attacker could otherwise select. The wire form, meaning the
  eight steps and their order, the hash and signature algorithms, and the
  canonical view, does not change, because evidence retained for years must
  remain checkable by the algorithm that produced it.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Arra
    given-names: Chaitanya Kumar
    affiliation: Nanorix Inc.
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0000-9986-8683"
